angerous situations, sees complex connections, excellent spatial vision, very good leadership, regarded as show-off, not open to new ideas, not good at taking criticism, unorganized, unfriendly.\n\nWe need to weigh safety-critical strengths vs interpersonal concerns. The panel's criteria include reliability, stress resilience, technical competence, decision quality, attention and information accuracy, crew cooperation, professional communication, responsibility, adaptability.\n\nB: reliability (very conscientious) strong. Stress resilience (handles stress well) strong. Technical competence (weather assessment, computer skills) good. Attention/information accuracy: weak memory for numbers (concern). Crew cooperation: makes nasty remarks (concern). Professional communication: same. Responsibility: not mentioned. Adaptability: not mentioned.\n\nC: reliability: not mentioned. Stress resilience: not mentioned. Technical competence: anticipates dangerous situations, sees complex connections, excellent spatial vision (strong). Decision quality: anticipates dangerous situations (good). Attention/information accuracy: unorganized (concern). Crew cooperation: leadership (good), but unfriendly, not open to new ideas, not good at taking criticism (concern). Professional communication: unfriendly, not open to new ideas (maybe). Responsibility: leadership (good). Adaptability: not open to new ideas (concern).\n\nThus both have strengths and concerns. B's concerns are communication and memory; C's concerns are adaptability, unorganized, unfriendly. Which is more critical? For long-distance flights, numeric accuracy is critical. B's weak memory for numbers could be high risk. C's unorganized could also affect attention. But we have no concrete evidence of B's memory causing errors; same for C's unorganized.\n\nWe need to decide vote. Could we ask a tool for more info? Perhaps ask Agent 1 or 3 for any concrete examples of C's unfriendly behavior or unorganized nature? But we already